<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Sacred · c. 250 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Song of Songs</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים</bdi> / <bdi lang="hbo-Latn">Šīr hašŠīrīm</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An ancient Hebrew love poem in the Bible, read in synagogues at Passover and long explained as a picture of God's love.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; traditionally ascribed to Solomon</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>ancient Israelites; Jewish and Christian worshippers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>dated by most scholars to the 3rd century BCE; estimates range from the 10th to the 2nd century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous, a book of the Hebrew Bible; traditionally ascribed to Solomon)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a>, also called the Canticle of Canticles or the Song of Solomon, is a biblical poem, one of the five megillot (&quot;scrolls&quot;) in the Ketuvim, the last section of the Tanakh. Unlike the other books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> it is erotic poetry: a woman and her lover speak in turn, express their desire, praise each other and invite each other to enjoy, through dialogues, dreams and metaphors, while the women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> form a chorus. The title follows an idiom of Scriptural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> for the greatest of its kind, as in &quot;Holy of Holies&quot;; &quot;Song of Solomon&quot; can mean a song of, by, for or dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. Modern scholarship tends to hold that the lovers are unmarried, and most scholars read the book as a celebration of human love rather than a divine metaphor.</p><p>The poem names no author, date or place of origin, and its superscription calling it &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s&quot; cannot be read as strictly as a modern statement of authorship. Since the 1800s most scholars have dated it to the 3rd century BCE. The strongest evidence is its language: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> gradually replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> after the end of the Babylonian exile in the late 6th century BCE, and its vocabulary, forms and syntax point to a date centuries after Solomon. It has parallels with Mesopotamian and Egyptian love poetry of the first half of the 1st millennium BCE and with the pastoral idylls of the Greek poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a>, so estimates range from the 10th to the 2nd century BCE. Whether it is one poem or an anthology is still debated. It seems to be rooted in festive performance; connections have been proposed with the &quot;sacred marriage&quot; of Ishtar and Tammuz, and Marvin H. Pope described it as a liturgy of the fertility cults of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. Other scholars find in it features of wisdom literature.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was accepted into the Jewish canon read as an allegory of the love between God and Israel, a view dominant for more than a thousand years, and its place in scripture was tied to that reading and to its attribution to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabbi-akiva">Rabbi Akiva</a>, in the 1st and 2nd centuries, forbade its use in popular celebrations, saying that whoever sang it in wine taverns as a vulgar song lost his share in the world to come, yet he defended it as the &quot;Holy of Holies&quot; of the Writings. Jewish mysticism after the Zohar of the 13th century gave it a central place, and &quot;Lecha Dodi&quot;, a 16th-century liturgical song, takes many passages from it, among them its opening two words. The whole Song is read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> in synagogues during the intermediate days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, often from a scroll like a Torah scroll; the custom of reading it at Passover was first recorded in 8th-century Jund Filastin. Some read it at the end of the Passover Seder or before the Sabbath.</p><p>Christian interpretation as a picture of God's love for his people began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a>. Gregory of Nyssa wrote fifteen Homilies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, comparing the bride to the soul and the groom to God, and Augustine, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a>, read it as the wedding of Christ and the Church. Theodore of Mopsuestia read it only literally, as an erotic poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> to the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>; his view was condemned at the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but from the 18th century a literal reading became the dominant one among biblical scholars. In the 12th century the bride was also read as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. Outside these traditions, Ibn Hazm (994–1064) dismissed the book, and in 1869 Syed Ahmad Khan argued that a word in its fifth chapter refers to Muhammad; in modern times feminist critics, beginning with Phyllis Trible, have made it an exemplary text.</p><p>Composers have set its words for centuries: Palestrina published 29 five-part motets on it in 1584, and there are settings by Francesca Caccini (1618), Heinrich Schütz (1629), Dieterich Buxtehude (1680) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">J. S. Bach</a>, Ralph Vaughan Williams's Flos Campi (1925), each movement headed by one of its verses, and many later works. Marc Chagall painted a five-picture cycle on it between 1957 and 1966, now in the Marc Chagall Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>; Lillian Hellman took the title of The Little Foxes (1939) from it, John Steinbeck named a character of The Grapes of Wrath (1939) Rose of Sharon after one of its epithets, and Toni Morrison called her 1977 novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Solomon</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A cycle of love poems in which a woman and a man call to each other, praise each other's beauty in images drawn from gardens, vineyards, flocks and the places of the land, seek each other through the city by night, and warn the women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> not to wake love before its time. It closes by setting the strength of love against death itself. The mood is passionate, playful and full of longing; Jewish and Christian tradition read it as an allegory of the love between God and his people.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁ֥יר הַשִּׁירִ֖ים אֲשֶׁ֥ר לִשְׁלֹמֹֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יִשָּׁקֵ֙נִי֙ מִנְּשִׁיק֣וֹת פִּ֔יהוּ כִּֽי־טוֹבִ֥ים דֹּדֶ֖יךָ מִיָּֽיִן׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לְרֵ֙יחַ֙ שְׁמָנֶ֣יךָ טוֹבִ֔ים שֶׁ֖מֶן תּוּרַ֣ק שְׁמֶ֑ךָ עַל־כֵּ֖ן עֲלָמ֥וֹת אֲהֵבֽוּךָ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מׇשְׁכֵ֖נִי אַחֲרֶ֣יךָ נָּר֑וּצָה הֱבִיאַ֨נִי הַמֶּ֜לֶךְ חֲדָרָ֗יו נָגִ֤ילָה וְנִשְׂמְחָה֙ בָּ֔ךְ נַזְכִּ֤ירָה דֹדֶ֙יךָ֙ מִיַּ֔יִן מֵישָׁרִ֖ים אֲהֵבֽוּךָ׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שְׁחוֹרָ֤ה אֲנִי֙ וְֽנָאוָ֔ה בְּנ֖וֹת יְרוּשָׁלָ֑͏ִם כְּאׇהֳלֵ֣י קֵדָ֔ר כִּירִיע֖וֹת שְׁלֹמֹֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אַל־תִּרְא֙וּנִי֙ שֶׁאֲנִ֣י שְׁחַרְחֹ֔רֶת שֶׁשְּׁזָפַ֖תְנִי הַשָּׁ֑מֶשׁ בְּנֵ֧י אִמִּ֣י נִֽחֲרוּ־בִ֗י שָׂמֻ֙נִי֙ נֹטֵרָ֣ה אֶת־הַכְּרָמִ֔ים כַּרְמִ֥י שֶׁלִּ֖י לֹ֥א נָטָֽרְתִּי׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הַגִּ֣ידָה לִּ֗י שֶׁ֤אָֽהֲבָה֙ נַפְשִׁ֔י אֵיכָ֣ה תִרְעֶ֔ה אֵיכָ֖ה תַּרְבִּ֣יץ בַּֽצׇּהֳרָ֑יִם שַׁלָּמָ֤ה אֶֽהְיֶה֙ כְּעֹ֣טְיָ֔ה עַ֖ל עֶדְרֵ֥י חֲבֵרֶֽיךָ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אִם־לֹ֤א תֵֽדְעִי֙ לָ֔ךְ הַיָּפָ֖ה בַּנָּשִׁ֑ים צְֽאִי־לָ֞ךְ בְּעִקְבֵ֣י הַצֹּ֗אן וּרְעִי֙ אֶת־גְּדִיֹּתַ֔יִךְ עַ֖ל מִשְׁכְּנ֥וֹת הָרֹעִֽים׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לְסֻֽסָתִי֙ בְּרִכְבֵ֣י פַרְעֹ֔ה דִּמִּיתִ֖יךְ רַעְיָתִֽי׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נָאו֤וּ לְחָיַ֙יִךְ֙ בַּתֹּרִ֔ים צַוָּארֵ֖ךְ בַּחֲרוּזִֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תּוֹרֵ֤י זָהָב֙ נַֽעֲשֶׂה־לָּ֔ךְ עִ֖ם נְקֻדּ֥וֹת הַכָּֽסֶף׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַד־שֶׁ֤הַמֶּ֙לֶךְ֙ בִּמְסִבּ֔וֹ נִרְדִּ֖י נָתַ֥ן רֵיחֽוֹ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">צְר֨וֹר הַמֹּ֤ר ׀ דּוֹדִי֙ לִ֔י בֵּ֥ין שָׁדַ֖י יָלִֽין׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֶשְׁכֹּ֨ל הַכֹּ֤פֶר ׀ דּוֹדִי֙ לִ֔י בְּכַרְמֵ֖י עֵ֥ין גֶּֽדִי׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הִנָּ֤ךְ יָפָה֙ רַעְיָתִ֔י הִנָּ֥ךְ יָפָ֖ה עֵינַ֥יִךְ יוֹנִֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הִנְּךָ֨ יָפֶ֤ה דוֹדִי֙ אַ֣ף נָעִ֔ים אַף־עַרְשֵׂ֖נוּ רַעֲנָנָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">קֹר֤וֹת בָּתֵּ֙ינוּ֙ אֲרָזִ֔ים רחיטנו רַהִיטֵ֖נוּ בְּרוֹתִֽים׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֲנִי֙ חֲבַצֶּ֣לֶת הַשָּׁר֔וֹן שׁוֹשַׁנַּ֖ת הָעֲמָקִֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כְּשֽׁוֹשַׁנָּה֙ בֵּ֣ין הַחוֹחִ֔ים כֵּ֥ן רַעְיָתִ֖י בֵּ֥ין הַבָּנֽוֹת׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כְּתַפּ֙וּחַ֙ בַּעֲצֵ֣י הַיַּ֔עַר כֵּ֥ן דּוֹדִ֖י בֵּ֣ין הַבָּנִ֑ים בְּצִלּוֹ֙ חִמַּ֣דְתִּי וְיָשַׁ֔בְתִּי וּפִרְי֖וֹ מָת֥וֹק לְחִכִּֽי׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הֱבִיאַ֙נִי֙ אֶל־בֵּ֣ית הַיָּ֔יִן וְדִגְל֥וֹ עָלַ֖י אַהֲבָֽה׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">סַמְּכ֙וּנִי֙ בָּאֲשִׁישׁ֔וֹת רַפְּד֖וּנִי בַּתַּפּוּחִ֑ים כִּי־חוֹלַ֥ת אַהֲבָ֖ה אָֽנִי׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שְׂמֹאלוֹ֙ תַּ֣חַת לְרֹאשִׁ֔י וִימִינ֖וֹ תְּחַבְּקֵֽנִי׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הִשְׁבַּ֨עְתִּי אֶתְכֶ֜ם בְּנ֤וֹת יְרוּשָׁלַ֙͏ִם֙ בִּצְבָא֔וֹת א֖וֹ בְּאַיְל֣וֹת הַשָּׂדֶ֑ה אִם־תָּעִ֧ירוּ ׀ וְֽאִם־תְּע֥וֹרְﬞר֛וּ אֶת־הָאַהֲבָ֖ה עַ֥ד שֶׁתֶּחְפָּֽץ׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">ק֣וֹל דּוֹדִ֔י הִנֵּה־זֶ֖ה בָּ֑א מְדַלֵּג֙ עַל־הֶ֣הָרִ֔ים מְקַפֵּ֖ץ עַל־הַגְּבָעֽוֹת׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">דּוֹמֶ֤ה דוֹדִי֙ לִצְבִ֔י א֖וֹ לְעֹ֣פֶר הָאַיָּלִ֑ים הִנֵּה־זֶ֤ה עוֹמֵד֙ אַחַ֣ר כׇּתְלֵ֔נוּ מַשְׁגִּ֙יחַ֙ מִן־הַֽחַלֹּנ֔וֹת מֵצִ֖יץ מִן־הַחֲרַכִּֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עָנָ֥ה דוֹדִ֖י וְאָ֣מַר לִ֑י ק֥וּמִי לָ֛ךְ רַעְיָתִ֥י יָפָתִ֖י וּלְכִי־לָֽךְ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כִּֽי־הִנֵּ֥ה הַסְּתָ֖ו עָבָ֑ר הַגֶּ֕שֶׁם חָלַ֖ף הָלַ֥ךְ לֽוֹ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הַנִּצָּנִים֙ נִרְא֣וּ בָאָ֔רֶץ עֵ֥ת הַזָּמִ֖יר הִגִּ֑יעַ וְק֥וֹל הַתּ֖וֹר נִשְׁמַ֥ע בְּאַרְצֵֽנוּ׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הַתְּאֵנָה֙ חָנְטָ֣ה פַגֶּ֔יהָ וְהַגְּפָנִ֥ים ׀ סְמָדַ֖ר נָ֣תְנוּ רֵ֑יחַ ק֥וּמִי לכי לָ֛ךְ רַעְיָתִ֥י יָפָתִ֖י וּלְכִי־לָֽךְ׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יוֹנָתִ֞י בְּחַגְוֵ֣י הַסֶּ֗לַע בְּסֵ֙תֶר֙ הַמַּדְרֵגָ֔ה הַרְאִ֙ינִי֙ אֶת־מַרְאַ֔יִךְ הַשְׁמִיעִ֖נִי אֶת־קוֹלֵ֑ךְ כִּֽי־קוֹלֵ֥ךְ עָרֵ֖ב וּמַרְאֵ֥יךְ נָאוֶֽה׃</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אֶֽחֱזוּ־לָ֙נוּ֙ שֻֽׁעָלִ֔ים שֻׁעָלִ֥ים קְטַנִּ֖ים מְחַבְּלִ֣ים כְּרָמִ֑ים וּכְרָמֵ֖ינוּ סְמָדַֽר׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">דּוֹדִ֥י לִי֙ וַאֲנִ֣י ל֔וֹ הָרֹעֶ֖ה בַּשּׁוֹשַׁנִּֽים׃</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַ֤ד שֶׁיָּפ֙וּחַ֙ הַיּ֔וֹם וְנָ֖סוּ הַצְּלָלִ֑ים סֹב֩ דְּמֵה־לְךָ֨ דוֹדִ֜י לִצְבִ֗י א֛וֹ לְעֹ֥פֶר הָאַיָּלִ֖ים עַל־הָ֥רֵי בָֽתֶר׃</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The song of songs, which is Solomon's.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for thy love is better than wine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Because of the savour of thy good ointments thy name is as ointment poured forth, therefore do the virgins love thee.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Draw me, we will run after thee: the king hath brought me into his chambers: we will be glad and rejoice in thee, we will remember thy love more than wine: the upright love thee.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am black, but comely, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, as the tents of Kedar, as the curtains of Solomon.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look not upon me, because I am black, because the sun hath looked upon me: my mother's children were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards; but mine own vineyard have I not kept.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tell me, O thou whom my soul loveth, where thou feedest, where thou makest thy flock to rest at noon: for why should I be as one that turneth aside by the flocks of thy companions?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>If thou know not, O thou fairest among women, go thy way forth by the footsteps of the flock, and feed thy kids beside the shepherds' tents.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have compared thee, O my love, to a company of horses in Pharaoh's chariots.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thy cheeks are comely with rows of jewels, thy neck with chains of gold.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will make thee borders of gold with studs of silver.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>While the king sitteth at his table, my spikenard sendeth forth the smell thereof.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>A bundle of myrrh is my wellbeloved unto me; he shall lie all night betwixt my breasts.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My beloved is unto me as a cluster of camphire in the vineyards of En-gedi.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold, thou art fair, my love; behold, thou art fair; thou hast doves' eyes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold, thou art fair, my beloved, yea, pleasant: also our bed is green.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The beams of our house are cedar, and our rafters of fir.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am the rose of Sharon, and the lily of the valleys.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As the lily among thorns, so is my love among the daughters.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As the apple tree among the trees of the wood, so is my beloved among the sons. I sat down under his shadow with great delight, and his fruit was sweet to my taste.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He brought me to the banqueting house, and his banner over me was love.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Stay me with flagons, comfort me with apples: for I am sick of love.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>His left hand is under my head, and his right hand doth embrace me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The voice of my beloved! behold, he cometh leaping upon the mountains, skipping upon the hills.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My beloved is like a roe or a young hart: behold, he standeth behind our wall, he looketh forth at the windows, shewing himself through the lattice.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My beloved spake, and said unto me, Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For, lo, the winter is past, the rain is over and gone;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing of birds is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in our land;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The fig tree putteth forth her green figs, and the vines with the tender grape give a good smell. Arise, my love, my fair one, and come away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my dove, that art in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs, let me see thy countenance, let me hear thy voice; for sweet is thy voice, and thy countenance is comely.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Take us the foxes, the little foxes, that spoil the vines: for our vines have tender grapes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My beloved is mine, and I am his: he feedeth among the lilies.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, turn, my beloved, and be thou like a roe or a young hart upon the mountains of Bether.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Song of Songs 1, Hebrew Bible (Masoretic text). Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%99%D7%A8_%D7%94%D7%A9%D7%99%D7%A8%D7%99%D7%9D_%D7%90" target="_blank" rel="noopener">שיר השירים א</a>, <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%99%D7%A8_%D7%94%D7%A9%D7%99%D7%A8%D7%99%D7%9D_%D7%91" target="_blank" rel="noopener">שיר השירים ב</a>); public domain.</p><p>English: King James Version (1611), Song of Solomon 1. Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)%2FSong_of_Solomon" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bible (King James)/Song of Solomon</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
